From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alejandro Garcia <skipper.pelygro@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 17:10:24 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: exynos: google: gs201: give the CPU levels a
 power model

The CPU nodes have a dynamic-power-coefficient, but the operating points
have no opp-microvolt, since the ACPM firmware sets the rail voltages.
dev_pm_opp_of_register_em() then registers the energy model with
dev_pm_opp_calc_power(), which fails on the first level's zero voltage,
so every boot logs "EM: invalid perf. state: -22" once per cluster and
no energy model exists (issue #182).

Give every CPU level its power instead, with opp-microwatt, which
dev_pm_opp_of_register_em() prefers. The values are the energy model
Google ships for gs201's B0 IPOP package: the default profile of
pixel_em_table in gs201-b0_v2-ipop.dts (kernel/devices/google/gs201,
branch android-gs-pantah-6.1-android16), per CPU, at exactly these 11,
15 and 18 levels. On the B0 package these phones use (gs201-b0.dtb),
stock computes the model from the same coefficients and each chip's
fused voltages, which the ACPM firmware reports, so these values are
one chip's: a stand-in with the same shape as stock's model, until the
voltages are read from the firmware. Drop dynamic-power-coefficient,
which nothing reads then.

With a valid energy model, asymmetric CPU capacities and schedutil, the
scheduler turns energy-aware scheduling on, as stock runs.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5
Signed-off-by: Alejandro Garcia <skipper.pelygro@gmail.com>
